TELNETD(8)          UNIX Programmer's Manual          TELNETD(8)



NAME
     telnetd - DARPA TELNET protocol server

SYNOPSIS
     telnetd [-i | port]

DESCRIPTION
     Telnetd serves one TELNET session: it listens on a TCP port,
     accepts a connection, allocates a pseudo-terminal pair, starts
     getty(8) on the slave side, and relays bytes between the
     connection and the pty until either end closes.  The login
     prompt, authentication and shell all come from getty(8) and
     login(1); telnetd itself carries bytes and negotiates options.

     Port is the TCP port to listen on, 23 by default.

     -i      Run as an inetd(8) child: the connection has already
             been accepted and handed over as standard input,
             output and error, so telnetd binds nothing and serves
             that connection directly.  This is the way telnetd is
             normally started on other systems, and it is how
             /etc/inetd.conf starts it here.  Nothing is written to
             standard output in this mode, because standard output
             IS the connection.

     Started by hand, telnetd binds 127.0.0.1, so it serves this
     machine only -- useful for reaching a second session from
     another virtual console: start telnetd on one console and run
     `telnet localhost' from another.  For logins from the network,
     run it under inetd(8), which owns the listening socket and can
     bind any address.

     ONE SESSION, THEN EXIT.  Telnetd accepts a single connection
     and exits when that session ends.  That is not a shortcut: on
     the W5100 path accept(2) DETACHES the listener, handing its
     hardware socket to the accepted connection, so a listener is
     single-use and a server must re-create it for the next client.
     The loopback transport mirrors that deliberately so the same
     shape works on both.  Run telnetd again for each session, or
     from a loop.

     Option negotiation follows the server side of the protocol:
     telnetd offers WILL ECHO and WILL SUPPRESS-GO-AHEAD, and asks
     the client DO TERMINAL-TYPE and DO NAWS.  Every other option
     is refused with a well-formed DONT or WONT rather than with
     silence, so a client asking for LINEMODE gets an answer.

     The terminal type is asked for and WAITED FOR before the
     session is started, because the name has to be known while
     there is still an argument list to put it in: telnetd passes
     it to getty(8) as -T type, getty passes it on to login(1),
     and login prefers it over the type in ttys(5).  So a session
     runs as the terminal the client says it is rather than as the
     pty line's entry.  A client that does not answer within two
     seconds keeps the ttys(5) type, which is what every session
     got before this existed.

     Diagnostics go to syslog(3), which on FGX writes to standard
     error rather than to a logging daemon.  Telnetd also prints
     `telnetd: listening', `telnetd: session on /dev/ttypN [type]'
     and `telnetd: session ended' to standard output as it goes.
     Empty brackets mean the client never named its terminal.

FILES
     /testbin/telnetd    the server itself; see BUGS
     /bin/getty          started on the pty slave
     /dev/ptypN          pty master claimed for the session
     /dev/ttypN          pty slave the session runs on

SEE ALSO
     telnet(1), inetd(8), getty(8), login(1), pty(4)

BUGS
     Started by hand, the listen address is 127.0.0.1 and cannot be
     changed, so telnetd itself does not accept logins from the
     network.  Under inetd(8) it does: inetd owns the listening
     socket, and the wildcard telnet line in /etc/inetd.conf serves
     the LAN and this machine alike.

     The peer host name is not recorded: telnetd does not call
     getpeername(2), so getty(8) is started without the -h
     argument BSD passes it.

     Telnetd is a reimplementation following 2.11BSD rather than a
     port of it, so much of what upstream does is simply absent --
     output buffering and urgent-data handling among it.  See
     apple/fgx#143.

     The terminal type reaches login(1) as an argument rather than
     in the environment.  Upstream sets TERM in the child and execs
     `login -p' to have it preserved; FGX's spawnx(2) carries file
     actions with an empty environment, so there is no environment
     to set.

     A session at a time.  A second client cannot connect while one
     is running, and telnetd exits rather than looping.

     Telnetd ships as /testbin/telnetd rather than in /usr/sbin,
     and /testbin is not on the default path, so it must be invoked
     by its full name.  It moves when it stops being staged as a
     test binary.



